{"kind":"assert","text":"OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE OF THIS CHANGE, found while verifying the request rather than after merging. Hugh needs to know this before the new binary is built, not after the resident fails to come up.\n\nThe resident running on this machine right now was launched as:\n\n  ./bin/gs serve --repo . --listen 127.0.0.1:7777 --acknowledge-trusted-processes\n\nOnce the flag is deleted, Go's flag package refuses an unknown flag outright — that exact command line will fail with \"flag provided but not defined: -acknowledge-trusted-processes\" and the resident will not start. So this change is not backward compatible for anyone whose launch script still passes it, and the failure lands at restart time, which is exactly when the resident is least convenient to debug. Every gitseq write in this workroom goes through that resident.\n\nThe deployment order is therefore the reverse of the usual one. Normally we restart the resident on the new binary first. Here the launch command must be edited to drop the argument in the same step as the binary is rebuilt, or the restart fails. Anyone with the flag in a shell alias, a login script or a terminal-history recall will hit it.\n\nI am recording this rather than designing around it, because designing around it would defeat the request. The obvious softening — accept the flag and ignore it — would leave a documented no-op flag in the surface, which is the same ceremony the request exists to remove, and condition 3 forbids it explicitly: grep for the flag must return nothing outside .git. Hugh's own framing was that running the service is itself sufficient acknowledgement, so a silently-tolerated vestige contradicts the instruction as much as keeping the gate would. I am implementing the hard removal as asked.\n\nWhat I have verified independently rather than taken from the request: the flag has exactly two occurrences in cmd/gs/main.go, the declaration at :1658 and the refusal at :1667-1668, and the variable acknowledgeTrustedProcesses appears at those two lines and nowhere else in the tree. So nothing branches on it, nothing logs it, and it grants and withholds no authority — removing it takes nothing away. Twenty-five references exist across Go and Markdown in total, all of them either those two lines, tests that pass the argument, or documentation that explains or invokes it.\n\nThe disclosure survives untouched, which is the part that actually matters for security. service.TrustedProcessPosture at internal/service/server.go:39 is unconditional, printed on every successful start at cmd/gs/main.go:1720, and carried as TrustBoundary in the status and summary payloads. None of that is being changed.\n\nFor hugh, concretely: after this merges and you rebuild, restart the resident with the argument dropped —\n\n  ./bin/gs serve --repo . --listen 127.0.0.1:7777\n\nand check for the flag in any alias or script before rebuilding. I will repeat this in the review request so the reviewer weighs the compatibility break deliberately rather than discovering it."}